  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report focuses on two aspects: (1) the higher amount in Lottery B (6000€ vs. 3000€), which is acknowledged but downplayed, and (2) the much higher probability of winning in Lottery A (90% vs. 45%). The individual explicitly states a preference for going with a 'high probability' of getting 3000€, indicating that the high chance of a positive outcome is the main driver of the decision. The justification centers on the likelihood of winning, not on the shape of the distribution or the concentration of risk on the downside. There is no mention of skewness, distributional asymmetry, or unfavorable outcomes carrying higher probability as a desirable feature. The deliberation reflects a preference for high-probability gains, not for right-skewed risks.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying which lottery is more right-skewed (i.e., assigns more probability to unfavorable outcomes) and preferring that one. Lottery B is more right-skewed, so the reason would select B. However, the individual chooses A and justifies this based on the high probability of winning 3000€, which reflects a preference for high-probability favorable outcomes—essentially the opposite of favoring right skewness. The individual considers probabilities and outcomes but interprets them in terms of security and likelihood of gain, not in terms of distributional skew. There is no alignment between the information used by the individual (favorable outcome probability) and the information central to the decision reason (unfavorable outcome probability concentration). Similarly, the justification (wanting a high chance to win) contradicts the logic of preferring right skewness. Therefore, it is certain that the individual did not use the skewness-based reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
